#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175818 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175968 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2737d1a7-2af9-54ed-8c42-5191fc68a31b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method"}] :question/id #uuid "7520ab43-c5b9-541b-80a9-4af1b91b2428" :question/math-academy-id "e-8855" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=3y-y^2$ and $x=y$, as shown below. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7e/7eca2a7eecfb9555a7e1555555a6d7b435a794e5715537d4fe94998ef00d124a.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let\n\n$$\nf(y)=3y-y^2\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=y$.\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= g(y) \\\\\n3y - y^{2} &= y \\\\\n2y - y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\ny(2 - y) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2$. So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2$.\n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/86/861a1300845e3832a201dd00410d462350f33a4654332b6b6fa7140dc6476dbb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y$, and\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=3y-y^2$.\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}y([right] - [left])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}y([3y - y^{2}] - [y])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}y(2y - y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{2}(2y^{2} - y^{3})dy \\\\\n&= 2π(2\\int_{0}^{2}y^{2}dy - \\int_{0}^{2}y^{3}dy) \\\\\n&= 2π(2 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right|_{0}^{2} - \\left.\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2π(2(\\frac{8}{3} - 0) - (\\frac{16}{4} - 0)) \\\\\n&= 2π(\\frac{16}{3} - 4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175819 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cd62b9c-0fbc-5a1e-b923-b2be8a55e44a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/id #uuid "9f7f3606-5c49-51d0-b4e5-cffd0310b6c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8857" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=2-y^2$, $x=y$, and the $x$-axis, as shown below. Using the shell method, find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/fa/fa2a775faed9c425a3b94e417b761d11486b4f41dae3129d9c9e016c28a652f0.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let\n\n$$\nf(y)=2-y^2\n$$\n\nand $g(y)=y$.\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= g(y) \\\\\n2 - y^{2} &= y \\\\\ny^{2} + y - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(y + 2)(y - 1) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= -2, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1$. So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/4c/4cc38a21168a86d2b64289e9c7877fbff4bf976b7214ed4befeb9644894f6172.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y$, and\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=2-y^2$.\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π\\int_{c}^{d}y([right] - [left])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}y([2 - y^{2}] - [y])dy \\\\\n&= 2π\\int_{0}^{1}y(2 - y - y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cd62b9c-0fbc-5a1e-b923-b2be8a55e44a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba2acd00-9b53-53b6-ab77-4f405beaee41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}y(4y - 2y^{2})dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174677} {:answer/id #uuid "d71365f0-76a4-5135-8feb-bb4b94aaac69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}y(2y^{2} - 4y)^{2}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174678} {:answer/id #uuid "d8db48f2-8530-5ffc-b6fe-aa0cd877d5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}y(6y - 2y^{2})dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174679} {:answer/id #uuid "da491968-8279-58a2-8f9e-4f73a8cbab30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}y(2y^{2} - 4y)dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174680} {:answer/id #uuid "36e48c5d-45b8-5f5e-8a05-a5164f6ff937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}(4y - 2y^{2})^{2}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174681}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ab089e7c-7f52-5582-ac6f-752c4f8bf60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174676}] :question/id #uuid "2289657c-a9c3-56eb-97a8-f3d9729a0b73" :question/math-academy-id "q-17023" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a5a3a246f2ecb4bee6cfc46f34d14b5abc9fcf22ed0d6173ed465bfab2cfeca6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x = 5y - 2y^{2}$ and $x = y$, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis."}]}
